---
title: "Finding Near-Duplicates with SimHash"
author: Lateralus contributors
date: 2026-11-28
abstract: Use the SimHash standard-library module to fingerprint text and find near-duplicates with a small Hamming distance.
---

# The idea

SimHash turns a document into a 64-bit fingerprint. Each token contributes the bits of its XXH64 hash to 64 running counters. A positive counter sets that bit in the final fingerprint. Similar documents therefore tend to differ in only a few bits.

The implementation tokenises case-insensitively, so punctuation and capitalisation do not change the example below.

## Fingerprint and compare

Load `stdlib/xxhash.ltl` and `stdlib/simhash.ltl`, then call `fingerprint` and `hamming`:

let original = "The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog"
let copy = "THE QUICK BROWN FOX JUMPS OVER THE LAZY DOG"
let a = fingerprint(original)
let b = fingerprint(copy)

print(hamming(a, b))       // 0
print(near_duplicate(original, copy, 3))  // true

`hamming` counts the differing bits. A threshold is an application decision: a small value is precise, while a larger value catches more variation and may produce more false positives. The runnable version is in `examples/simhash.ltl`.

## Stable vectors

The test vectors pin the XXH64 anchors `"" = 0xEF46DB3751D8E999` and `"abc" = 0x44BC2CF5AD770999`, plus complete SimHash fingerprints. Keeping these values in tests makes accidental changes to tokenisation or hash wiring visible.

